prompt:
The marking gang finished a small crossroads last night and the handover photograph has to go to the highway authority before the road opens.

What is on the ground:

  - two approach lanes that meet at right angles, one running up the picture and one running across it, each with its own stop line;
  - in each of those two lanes, one legend in block capitals laid in white thermoplastic, and beside the legend a direction arrow of the ordinary kind;
  - the gang's setting-out card, weighted down on the kerb;
  - cones still standing at the stop lines.

The standard the gang works to says this much, and it is not mine to change: a legend on a carriageway is laid several times longer than it is wide, because a driver reads it at a very shallow angle, and the direction it is lengthened in is the direction that lane's traffic travels.

Which way each of the two legends actually ends up lengthened is what the handover photograph is for, and I am telling you neither. Fly the drone directly over the middle of the junction and photograph straight down, high enough that both approaches, both legends and both arrows are inside one frame. Keep the paint bare: no figure in millimetres, no ratio, no note of any kind written on the carriageway or added to the photograph afterwards.
